  "final_text": "Wrote `impact.txt` at the repo root with the five definitions whose bodies call `resolve_color_default`:\n\n- `src/click/exceptions.py#ClickException.__init__` \u2014 caches `self.show_color = resolve_color_default()`\n- `src/click/termui.py#_readline_prompt`, `#get_pager_file`, `#progressbar`\n- `src/click/utils.py#echo`\n\nHow I established it: `who` on `github.com/pallets/click@36baa15ff831/src/click/globals.py#resolve_color_default` returned 9 backlinks over a complete crawl (166 files, `complete: true`, `unresolved_matching: 0`). I dropped the three module-level entries (`exceptions.py`, `termui.py`, `utils.py` at line 1 \u2014 bare imports) and the `ClickException` class entry, which is just the class containing the `__init__` use (verified in `src/click/exceptions.py:44-49`); the class body itself only annotates `show_color`.\n\nNo test uses: the index reports none, and I confirmed by reading the plausible candidates (`tests/test_termui.py`, `tests/test_utils/test_echo.py`, `tests/test_compat.py`, `tests/conftest.py`) plus `CliRunner.isolation`, which patches `should_strip_ansi` rather than this function. One caveat worth stating: cttp does not treat a name reached through a string (e.g. `monkeypatch.setattr(\"click.globals.resolve_color_default\", ...)`) as a reference, and its crawl skipped 2 files it could not identify to me, so a string-based use in those would not appear \u2014 nothing in the files I read suggests one exists.",
